+### Rarefaction {#sec-rarefaction-alpha}
+
+To control uneven sampling depths, we can do rarefaction
+(see [@sec-rarefaction]). Rarefaction for alpha diversity metrics can be applied
+by specifying the optional arguments `sample` and `niter` in `addAlpha()`.
+`niter` controls the number of rarefaction iterations (choose a sufficiently
+large value for stable estimates). `sample` sets the rarefaction depth, i.e.,
+the number of reads sampled per iteration from each sample.
+
+```{r}
+#| label: alpha_rarefaction
+tse <- addAlpha(
+    tse,
+    assay.type = "counts",
+    index = "observed_richness",
+    name = "richness_w_rarefaction",
+    niter = 10
+)
+```
+
+Next compare the results with and without rarefaction.
+
+```{r}
+#| label: compare:rarefaction
+plotBoxplot(tse, col.var = "observed_richness", fill.by = "SampleType") +
+    plotBoxplot(tse, col.var = "richness_w_rarefaction", fill.by = "SampleType") +
+    plot_layout(guides = "collect")
+```
+
+These results illustrate that uneven sequencing depth affects the diversity
+estimates: applying rarefaction changes the values compared with the
+non-rarefied analysis, indicating that part of the apparent differences was
+driven by sampling effort rather than biology.
+
